{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/r1-correctness.json && printf '\\\\n====\\\\n' && sed -n '1,240p' docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/r1-safety_bypass.json && printf '\\\\n====\\\\n' && sed -n '1,260p' docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/r1-completeness_governance.json\"","aggregated_output":"{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":1065456,\"duration_api_ms\":1066014,\"num_turns\":24,\"result\":\"```json\\n{\\n  \\\"reviewer\\\": \\\"sonnet-correctness\\\",\\n  \\\"lens\\\": \\\"correctness\\\",\\n  \\\"slice\\\": \\\"WP-SCP-024-024B-extras\\\",\\n  \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"APPROVED_WITH_FINDINGS\\\",\\n  \\\"crit_count\\\": 0,\\n  \\\"maj_count\\\": 2,\\n  \\\"min_count\\\": 1,\\n  \\\"nit_count\\\": 0,\\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"024B-extras correctness review: 0 CRIT, 2 MAJ, 1 MIN. All four carry-forward findings (R8-CORR-001/002/003, R7-BOOTSTRAP) confirmed closed. The --restore mode GET→PUT shape transform is correct: Python helper strips all GET-only fields, coerces enforce_admins.enabled to boolean, handles null required_status_checks, and compacts restrictions arrays. APPLY_FAIL=0 wrap is correctly initialized before the apply block; failed PUT continues to log emission as specified. The four posture-degradation flags are correctly enforced by the script before apply. required_signatures sub-resource handling (POST/DELETE with 404 suppression) is correct. R8-CORR-002 is definitively closed: check_forward_mode_safety() is called only when RESTORE_MODE -eq 0, so prior-entry detection cannot run in restore context. The workflow path filter (024[C-Z]*/**) plus job-body short-circuit correctly excludes tooling slices without the paths-ignore approach. CORR-001 (MAJ): validate_expected_wrapper_sha_against_tags executes and emits a success log before the prior_restore_evidence_present guard fires; an operator sees 'SHA validated against release tag' immediately before an unrelated exit 2, which is misleading and wastes N+1 GitHub API calls. The test enable-expected-sha-cross-target explicitly asserts this misleading stdout is present, confirming the ordering is implemented but not intentionally designed. CORR-002 (MAJ): ADOPT-001 §12.8 Gate 1 documents only 2 of 4 posture-degradation flags; --i-understand-restore-re-enables-force-pushes and --i-understand-restore-re-enables-deletions are absent from the playbook. An operator restoring to a pre-state with allow_force_pushes=true would hit an unexplained exit 2 mid-incident, directly violating the <30-min SLO. The script correctly enforces both flags; only the documentation is missing. CORR-003 (MIN): run_workflow_guard_case uses an inline bash snippet that lacks the cohort-preference case statement and tooling short-circuit from the real YAML; mixed-PR scenarios and the 024[C-F] preference branch are untested.\\\",\\n  \\\"carry_forward_verification\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R8-CORR-001\\\",\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"CLOSED\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"check-invocation-log-entry.sh uses single-pass ^cascade-status:\\\\\\\\s+ regex anchored at line start with exactly-one-match assertion (exits 2 if count != 1). No trailing inline prose can be captured.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R8-CORR-002\\\",\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"CLOSED\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"check_forward_mode_safety() is invoked only inside the guard `if [ \\\\\\\"$RESTORE_MODE\\\\\\\" -eq 0 ]` (line 568-570 of enable-required-check.sh). All prior_restore_evidence_present calls and prior-entry detection logic are inside that guard. Restore-mode execution path cannot trigger spurious contradiction messages.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R8-CORR-003\\\",\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"CLOSED\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"Closed in 024B-core per DISPATCH-NOTE. TF-ticket match pattern in check-invocation-log-entry.sh uses spec-compliant slug canonicalisation.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R7-BOOTSTRAP\\\",\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"CLOSED\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"Workflow trigger uses paths: [docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024[C-Z]*/**] which structurally excludes 024A and 024B*. Job-body resolve-dispatch step additionally short-circuits exit 0 for tooling slices (024A, 024B-core, 024B-extras, 024G). Combined, this is functionally equivalent to the paths-ignore approach described in DISPATCH-NOTE and closes the bootstrap defect.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"findings\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CORR-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"validate_expected_wrapper_sha_against_tags is called before prior_restore_evidence_present guard; tag API calls run and success log emitted even when the invocation will immediately fail due to absent restore evidence.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"enable-required-check.sh lines 547-553: validate_expected_wrapper_sha_against_tags($EXPECTED_WRAPPER_SHA) executes unconditionally when --expected-wrapper-sha is set, emitting 'expected wrapper SHA validated against release tag <tag>' to stdout. The prior_restore_evidence_present check fires after, emitting the exit-2 error. Test enable-expected-sha-cross-target (test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh) explicitly asserts expected_stdout contains 'expected wrapper SHA validated' AND exit_code 2 — confirming this ordering is tested as-implemented, not as an intentional design choice.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Operator sees a misleading 'SHA validated against release tag' success message immediately before an unrelated failure. N+1 GitHub API calls (paginated tag list + per-tag object SHA) are wasted. The success log could cause an operator under time pressure to believe the critical Gate 3 step succeeded when the actual error is orthogonal.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Reorder: check prior_restore_evidence_present first; error immediately with 'no prior restore evidence found — --expected-wrapper-sha requires a prior --restore invocation on this repo' if absent; only proceed to validate_expected_wrapper_sha_against_tags if evidence is present. Update test enable-expected-sha-cross-target to assert expected_stdout is empty (or contains only the restore-evidence-missing error).\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CORR-002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"ADOPT-001 §12.8 Gate 1 documents only 2 of 4 posture-degradation acknowledgement flags; --i-understand-restore-re-enables-force-pushes and --i-understand-restore-re-enables-deletions are absent from the operator playbook.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"ADOPT-001 §12.8 lines 1205-1210 lists --i-understand-restore-removes-admin-enforcement and --i-understand-restore-removes-required-checks only. enable-required-check.sh enforces --i-understand-restore-re-enables-force-pushes (line 680) when pre-state allow_force_pushes is true and --i-understand-restore-re-enables-deletions (line 688) when pre-state allow_deletions is true. An operator following §12.8 verbatim on a repo whose captured pre-state has either flag enabled would receive an unexplained exit 2 with no documentation path to resolution.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Playbook-following operator cannot complete Gate 1 rollback; SLO <30 min from operator decision to merge-restored state is directly violated while the operator diagnoses the undocumented exit 2. Severity is MAJ not CRIT because the script behaviour is correct — only the prose documentation is incomplete.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add --i-understand-restore-re-enables-force-pushes and --i-understand-restore-re-enables-deletions to §12.8 Gate 1 flag table with same explanatory format as the existing two entries, noting each is required only when the captured pre-state JSON has the corresponding field set to true. No script changes required; tests already cover this path (test lines 2284-2289).\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CORR-003\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"run_workflow_guard_case test exercises an inline bash snippet that omits the cohort-preference logic and tooling short-circuit present in the real YAML; the 024[C-F] preference branch and mixed-PR scenarios are not tested.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh lines 157-224: run_workflow_guard_case runs an inline bash block (lines 171-186) that emits enforce=true for any dispatch note path, without the cohort-preference grep (024[C-F]/DISPATCH-NOTE.md) or tooling-slice case statement present in check-invocation-log-entry.yml lines 57-74. No test case provides a PR diff containing both a tooling dispatch note (e.g. 024B-extras/DISPATCH-NOTE.md) and a cohort dispatch note (e.g. 024C/DISPATCH-NOTE.md) to verify the preference logic selects the cohort note.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"A bug in the cohort-preference grep pattern would not be caught by the test suite. A mixed-PR scenario could silently enforce on a tooling dispatch note, causing spurious failures on PRs that touch both tooling docs and cohort docs.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add run_workflow_guard_case variants: (a) diff contains only a tooling dispatch note → verify job exits 0; (b) diff contains both tooling and cohort dispatch notes → verify cohort is selected and job proceeds to enforcement. Either extract the resolve-dispatch step logic into a sourced bash function testable in isolation, or add an act/nektos workflow-level test.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"2af16195-fba4-46c9-9b26-2c739c686ecd\",\"total_cost_usd\":1.82164905,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":20,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":149739,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":1314745,\"output_tokens\":37005,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":149739,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":3,\"output_tokens\":2373,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":7381,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":16139,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":16139},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":1753,\"outputTokens\":28,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.001893,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-sonnet-4-6\":{\"inputTokens\":1324,\"outputTokens\":54503,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":1455726,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":149739,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":1.81975605,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"089072a8-3ae9-4dfc-b64a-715b4bc008e5\"}\n\n====\n{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":986787,\"duration_api_ms\":987448,\"num_turns\":23,\"result\":\"I have enough information to produce the complete safety bypass review. Let me compose the JSON output now.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"reviewer\\\": \\\"sonnet-safety_bypass\\\",\\n  \\\"lens\\\": \\\"safety_bypass\\\",\\n  \\\"slice\\\": \\\"WP-SCP-024-024B-extras\\\",\\n  \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"APPROVED_WITH_FINDINGS\\\",\\n  \\\"crit_count\\\": 0,\\n  \\\"maj_count\\\": 2,\\n  \\\"min_count\\\": 3,\\n  \\\"nit_count\\\": 1,\\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"024B-extras SAFETY_BYPASS review: 0 CRIT, 2 MAJ, 3 MIN, 1 nit. Carry-forward R8-SAFE-001, R8-SAFE-002, and R8-BOOTSTRAP confirmed closed. APPLY_FAIL wrap is correctly structured: failed PUT sets FAIL=1 and falls through to log emission; exit 1 follows log; no early-exit path skips the log block after PUT is attempted. All four posture-degradation flags are checked before any PUT (force_pushes/deletions before APPLY_FAIL=0; admin enforcement and required checks inside the restore block before PUT call). Forward-mode safety check uses .path == '.github/workflows/policy-check-wrapper.yml' for workflow lookup, not display name — fake_gh test actively rejects branch= in workflow-runs URL confirming no branch filter leakage. Cold-start bypass (--i-understand-this-repo-has-no-prior-green-ci) is mutex with --expected-wrapper-sha and rejected when prior restore evidence exists. Tag validation issues real gh api git/ref/tags per-tag lookups, not shape-only checks; paginated tag-list confirmed by paginate-tags test. Working-tree prior-entry detection via git diff HEAD covers uncommitted Gate-1 entries, closing R8-SAFE-002. Two MAJ findings: (1) validate_restore_source_json checks required_status_checks is dict|None but does not validate that contexts is an array; a string value produces jq string-length (nonzero), bypassing the --i-understand-restore-removes-required-checks gate and suppressing the CAUTION audit-trail entries that document the consent; PUT subsequently fails 422 and APPLY_FAIL catches it, so no degradation occurs, but the consent-audit surface is circumvented. (2) prior_restore_evidence_present includes git log -p over full history; a Gate 1 restore entry is never erased from history, making the --expected-wrapper-sha requirement permanently sticky post-break-glass — neither D-047 nor §12.8 documents this permanence, so operators hitting unexpected exit 2 on subsequent forward-mode invocations will reach for --i-understand-no-gate-2-verification as a workaround, permanently weakening Gate 3 for that adopter.\\\",\\n  \\\"carry_forward_verification\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R8-SAFE-001\\\",\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"CLOSED\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"--i-understand-no-gate-2-verification is rejected by --restore (line 258-261) and --expected-wrapper-sha mutex (line 264). When used in forward mode it emits CAUTION to stdout at line 565 before any API call, emits CAUTION to the log block at line 882-883, and records operator login in the CAUTION text. ADOPT-001 §12.8 has both WARNING and CAUTION callouts for the escape hatch. D-047 rationale explicitly names the escape hatch. Auditable trail confirmed complete.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R8-SAFE-002\\\",\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"CLOSED\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"prior_restore_evidence_present (lines 458-485) concatenates committed_blob, working_tree_diff (git diff HEAD -- log_path), and committed_history_diff (git log -p -- log_path) before the awk scan. The git diff HEAD path captures staged + unstaged uncommitted changes. Gate 3 check_forward_mode_safety (line 539) calls this function for all forward-mode invocations, so an uncommitted Gate-1 log entry in the working tree triggers the --expected-wrapper-sha requirement before any PUT.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R8-BOOTSTRAP\\\",\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"CLOSED\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"CI workflow uses paths: [024[C-Z]*/**] allowlist; 024B-extras DISPATCH-NOTE path (024B-extras/) does not match [C-Z] range. For PRs that touch STATUS.md or branch-protection-log.md (both in paths:), the resolve-dispatch step case statement at line 66-72 explicitly short-circuits 024A, 024B-core, 024B-extras, and 024G to exit 0. The 024B-extras introducing PR cannot self-block enforcement.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"findings\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-MAJ-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"validate_restore_source_json does not validate that required_status_checks.contexts is an array; a string value produces jq string-length (nonzero), bypassing the --i-understand-restore-removes-required-checks posture gate and suppressing CAUTION audit entries.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"validate_restore_source_json (lines 346-412) checks required_types = {required_status_checks: (dict, NoneType), ...} — type-only, no inner-field validation. A before-JSON with {\\\\\\\"required_status_checks\\\\\\\": {\\\\\\\"contexts\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"policy-check\\\\\\\"}} passes validation. Posture check at line 723: jq -r '.required_status_checks.contexts // [] | length' returns string length of \\\\\\\"policy-check\\\\\\\" (12), not 0. Condition 'restore_contexts -eq 0' is false; --i-understand-restore-removes-required-checks is not required. Transform recurse on string returns the string unchanged; PUT body has contexts: \\\\\\\"policy-check\\\\\\\"; GitHub API returns 422; APPLY_FAIL=1. Log block emits no RESTORE_CAUTION_LINES for required-checks removal.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Consent-audit surface circumvented: operator can attempt restore to required-checks-absent posture without the acknowledgement flag and without CAUTION entries appearing in the audit log. APPLY_FAIL catches the 422 (no actual degradation), but auditors reviewing the log see only a failed PUT, not a suppressed consent confirmation. Affects any adopter whose pre-state JSON is manually constructed or contains a non-array contexts field.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add array-type validation in validate_restore_source_json for required_status_checks.contexts when required_status_checks is not None: verify isinstance(data['required_status_checks'].get('contexts'), (list, type(None))). Alternatively, normalise contexts to [] in the transform when the value is not a list. Add a test case with contexts: \\\\\\\"string\\\\\\\" asserting exit 2 with the posture-removal error.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-MAJ-002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"prior_restore_evidence_present includes git log -p over full history; a Gate 1 restore entry is never cleared from history, making --expected-wrapper-sha permanently required for all subsequent forward-mode invocations on that adopter. D-047 and §12.8 do not document this permanence.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"prior_restore_evidence_present (lines 466-467): committed_history_diff = git -C REPO_ROOT log -p -- log_path. After a successful break-glass cycle (Gate 1 → Gate 2 → Gate 3 with --expected-wrapper-sha), the restore log entry persists in git history. main has force-push disabled (D-030) and admin bypass off (D-032), so the commit cannot be expunged. Every subsequent forward-mode invocation hits check_forward_mode_safety → prior_restore_evidence_present returns true → line 540 demands --expected-wrapper-sha or Gate 2 bypass. D-047 row and §12.8 Gate 3 describe the requirement only for the immediate post-Gate-1 re-enable, implying it is transient.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Operators following §12.8 verbatim on a post-break-glass adopter will unexpectedly hit exit 2 on all future forward-mode invocations (e.g., version-bump re-enables, annual review re-enables). Under time pressure they will use --i-understand-no-gate-2-verification as a workaround, permanently weakening Gate 3 for that adopter. The CAUTION audit trail will accumulate indefinitely, degrading signal-to-noise for auditors.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add a sentence to §12.8 Gate 3 and D-047: 'After any break-glass cycle, all subsequent forward-mode invocations for the same adopter permanently require --expected-wrapper-sha because the restore evidence persists in git history. This is intentional.' Alternatively, introduce a --reset-restore-evidence flag (requires explicit ack + logs the reset) that adds a sentinel to the log file that prior_restore_evidence_present treats as a history-clear marker, eliminating the permanent stickiness without history rewriting.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-MIN-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"validate_restore_source_json does not validate that enforce_admins.enabled is a strict boolean; Python bool() coerces non-empty strings (including \\\\\\\"false\\\\\\\") to True, bypassing admin-removal posture check in the 'more secure' direction.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"Transform at lines 382-384: out[key] = bool(value.get('enabled', False)) for enforce_admins dict. Python bool('false') = True. A before-JSON with \\\\\\\"enforce_admins\\\\\\\": {\\\\\\\"enabled\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"false\\\\\\\"} produces RESTORE_PAYLOAD.enforce_admins = true. Posture check at line 719: RESTORE_TARGET_ADMINS_ENABLED = jq -r '.enforce_admins // false' on RESTORE_PAYLOAD = 'true'; condition 'RESTORE_TARGET_ADMINS_ENABLED = false' is false; --i-understand-restore-removes-admin-enforcement not required. PUT sets enforce_admins=true instead of false. Operator's intended restore (admin enforcement disabled) is not achieved without error.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Operator using a manually-crafted before-JSON with string 'false' for enabled will have their restore silently diverge from intent: admin enforcement remains ON. No security regression (more secure than intended), but the restore does not match the captured pre-state. Operator may believe break-glass is complete when the target repo's branch protection still differs from the documented pre-state.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add strict boolean check in validate_restore_source_json: verify isinstance(data['enforce_admins'].get('enabled'), bool) and emit error if not. This ensures the transform input is well-typed before bool() is applied.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-MIN-002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"prior_restore_evidence_present false-positive: the awk script cannot distinguish 'Restoring TO:' in the restore-block log header from 'Restoring TO:' embedded in forward-mode JSON payload content within a matching target block.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"prior_restore_evidence_present awk (lines 469-484): sets in_block=1 when header matches target, then sets found=1 when any subsequent line (after stripping +/- prefix) matches /Restoring TO:/. A forward-mode log entry for target jrnb2024/pim@main whose before/after JSON contains a check context named 'Restoring TO: old-gate' would trip the detector. The awk does not anchor the 'Restoring TO:' scan to the restore-specific '- **Restoring TO:**' markdown prefix; any substring match within an in-block line suffices.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"False-positive requires operator to supply --expected-wrapper-sha or --i-understand-no-gate-2-verification for a legitimate forward-mode invocation with no actual prior restore. Under time pressure the operator uses the Gate 2 bypass, producing spurious CAUTION entries in the audit log and weakening Gate 3 for that adopter. Unlikely in practice (requires 'Restoring TO:' in a check context name) but unmitigated and untested.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Tighten the awk pattern to match the exact markdown prefix used in restore log blocks: change 'line ~ /Restoring TO:/' to 'line ~ /^- \\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*Restoring TO:\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*/' (anchored to the markdown header format). Add a test case where the forward-mode log payload JSON contains 'Restoring TO:' as a context name substring and assert no prior-restore is detected.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-MIN-003\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"No test verifies the PUT body shape when the before-JSON has required_status_checks.contexts = null inside a non-null RSC dict; the transform passes null through, producing a potentially 422-causing PUT body after the operator provides the posture-ack flag.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"transform() (lines 377-410): for a non-null required_status_checks dict, the function recurses into it (else branch at line 403). If the inner dict has 'contexts': null, transform(None) returns None (base case at line 410). PUT body has {required_status_checks: {strict: ..., contexts: null}}. GitHub branch-protection PUT requires contexts to be an array, not null. Posture check (line 723) correctly fires because jq '.contexts // [] | length' on null = 0; operator provides --i-understand-restore-removes-required-checks; PUT proceeds; 422 returned; APPLY_FAIL=1. The test restore-null-status-checks (line 2338-2383) only covers required_status_checks = null (top-level null, not contexts: null inside a dict), which takes the transform's special-case branch and correctly emits {strict: false, contexts: []}.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"In transform(), when processing a required_status_checks dict, normalise contexts to [] if the value is None or not a list: 'if key == \\\\\\\"contexts\\\\\\\" and not isinstance(value, list): out[key] = []'. Add a test fixture with {required_status_checks: {strict: true, contexts: null}} and assert jq '.required_status_checks.contexts == []' on the PUT body.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-nit-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"nit\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"paths: STATUS.md in CI workflow triggers the enforcement job for every PR touching STATUS.md, including non-cascade PRs; only the job-body short-circuit prevents spurious failures, with no documentation that this is intentional.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"check-invocation-log-entry.yml lines 19-21: paths includes STATUS.md alongside the cascade-slice paths and branch-protection-log.md. A PR that modifies only STATUS.md (e.g., a solo status update with no cascade DISPATCH-NOTE) triggers the workflow; resolve-dispatch exits 0 ('No cascade-slice DISPATCH-NOTE found'). No comment in the workflow documents that STATUS.md is in paths to handle the onboarded-operator-bump case where STATUS.md + DISPATCH-NOTE appear together.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add an inline comment above the STATUS.md path entry explaining the requirement: '# STATUS.md: required for onboarded-operator-bump validation (cascade-status: onboarded-operator-bump slices must include a TF row in STATUS.md; the job short-circuits for non-cascade PRs)'. No code change required.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"d9935c78-d8b7-48c2-9be9-f63e4d879185\",\"total_cost_usd\":1.54669645,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":16,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":133567,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":974944,\"output_tokens\":50100,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":133567,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":1,\"output_tokens\":10839,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":137663,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":3285,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":3285},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":1669,\"outputTokens\":24,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.001789,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-sonnet-4-6\":{\"inputTokens\":16,\"outputTokens\":50100,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":974944,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":133567,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":1.54490745,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"3c9128f1-29e4-4fc9-a35e-3deed463d61d\"}\n\n====\n{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":525013,\"duration_api_ms\":523855,\"num_turns\":38,\"result\":\"I now have all the information needed to compile the review. Let me emit the structured findings report.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"lens\\\": \\\"completeness_governance\\\",\\n  \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"BLOCKED\\\",\\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"024B-extras completeness governance review: 1 MAJ, 2 MIN, 1 nit. DISPATCH-NOT