ip-evidence-PLACEHOLDER.md step 6: 'verify branch-protection API GET response matches captured before-state on key fields (strict, contexts, enforce_admins.enabled, required_signatures.enabled); API envelope fields (timestamps, _links, url) differ and should be excluded'.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"A reviewer auditing the step-7 evidence file against the DISPATCH-NOTE AC will use 'byte-for-byte' as the pass criterion and could reject a correct demonstration because envelope fields differ. The placeholder is the operator-facing instruction and is correct; the AC and TF row create a false standard that could block an otherwise valid evidence commit.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Update DISPATCH-NOTE AC step-7 text and TF-024B-STEP7-DEMO-001 STATUS.md row to match the placeholder's specification: 'verify key fields (strict, contexts, enforce_admins.enabled, required_signatures.enabled) match via jq comparison; API envelope fields excluded'.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CG-R1-MIN-002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"The workflow's cohort-priority grep uses `024[C-F]` (a closed range matching the four current cohort slices) while the paths trigger uses `024[C-Z]*/**` (an open range covering any future slice from C to Z). The mismatch creates an undocumented implicit assumption: any cohort slice added outside [C-F] (e.g., if 024G naming expands or a second cohort is numbered [H-Z]) falls through to head -n1 ordering instead of the priority-selection branch. No TF tracks this constraint.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"Workflow line 58: `grep -E '^docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024[C-F]/DISPATCH-NOTE.md$'`. Workflow paths trigger line 18: `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024[C-Z]*/**`. Plan-doc §6 scopes cohort to 024C/024D/024E/024F and tooling to 024G only, so [C-F] is currently correct. No TF documents the [C-F] assumption as a forward-compat constraint.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"No current defect: present cohort is [C-F]. Risk is forward-compat: a future plan amendment adding a cohort slice outside [C-F] would silently degrade priority selection on mixed PRs without any visible CI failure. The constraint is invisible to a future dispatch author reading only §6.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Either broaden the grep pattern to `024[C-Z]` to match the paths trigger, or add a DISPATCH-NOTE / plan-doc §6 note explicitly stating 'cohort slices must remain within 024C-024F; the CI workflow cohort-priority grep is scoped to this range and must be updated if the cohort expands'. File as TF or inline note.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CG-R1-nit-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"nit\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"No dedicated test asserts that forward-mode safety-check output is absent when running in --restore mode (the R8-CORR-002 regression guard). Restore-mode tests implicitly verify correct behavior but do not include an explicit negative assertion.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"DISPATCH-NOTE carry-forward: 'R8-CORR-002: prior-entry detection runs outside restore-mode guard — ensure restore-mode guard scoping is tight.' Implementation is correct (check_forward_mode_safety() called only inside `if [ \\\\\\\"$RESTORE_MODE\\\\\\\" -eq 0 ]` at line 568). Test file has run_restore_case and run_restore_admin_posture_case helpers but no case that asserts the absence of forward-mode safety log lines (e.g., 'safety check:') in restore-mode stdout.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add a restore-mode test case that asserts `grep -v 'safety check' stdout` or that the forward-mode safety workflow-lookup call is absent from the fake_gh calls.log, making R8-CORR-002 regression-detectable without manual inspection.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"2d9f6d00-1d3f-4bb8-bcd1-1674097d96cd\",\"total_cost_usd\":1.7630895999999996,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":1368,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":141898,\"cache_read_inpu