{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n \\\"expected-wrapper-sha|prior_restore_evidence_present|validate_restore_source_json|required_status_checks|i-understand-restore-re-enables-force-pushes|i-understand-restore-re-enables-deletions|Post-break-glass permanence|D-047|Gate 1|12\\\\.8\\\" scripts/enable-required-check.sh tests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh docs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md docs/DECISIONS.md docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/r1-correctness.json docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/r1-safety_bypass.json docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/r1-completeness_governance.json\"","aggregated_output":"docs/DECISIONS.md:3:**Last Updated:** 2026-05-10 (D-047 filed for WP-SCP-024 024B-extras; restore/break-glass/workflow wiring)\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:48:> **D-047 reservation note (2026-05-10):** D-047 was reserved for WP-SCP-024\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:49:> 024B-extras (`--restore` + ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass + CI workflow wiring);\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:96:| D-044 | 2026-05-09 | Adopt the WP-SCP-024 slice 024B-core operational contracts for cascade scaffolding. Components: (1) `scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh` (`SCP-073-scaffolder` per WP-SCP-020 §11 follow-up + WP-SCP-021 follow-up `SCP-075-scaffolder-compose`) generates adopter-side artefacts mechanically - bootstrap-only (refuses `CI=true` / `GITHUB_ACTIONS=true` per D-035 symmetry); validates `--scp-sha` against SCP main HEAD lineage (warns if not main HEAD; refuses if non-existent); emits a TF-023E-002 warning for post-v1.2.0 SHAs while recommending the v1.0.0 pin; `--scp-sha` 40-char hex regex; `--default-branch` enum {main, master, develop}; `--scorecard-emit` enum; `--output-dir` writability + path-normalised system-path denylist (incl `/private/var/*` macOS canonicalisation); emits `MANIFEST.json` with SHA256 audit; arguments per plan-doc §5.3. Canonical adopter slugs are normalization aids only, not collision-resistant IDs, so cohort repo names must remain unique after canonicalization. (2) `templates/adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl` is the canonical adopter wrapper shape per ADOPT-001 §12 with Renovate inline-comment marker `# renovate: datasource=github-tags depName=jrnb2024/standards-control-plane` enabling auto-bump per plan-doc invariant 8 Threshold A criterion; substitutions `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}`, `{{SCP_SHA}}`, `{{SCORECARD_EMIT}}`; least-privilege workflow-level permissions (`contents:read` + `statuses:write` only). (3) `scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh` CLI tool implements plan-doc invariant 2 cascade-status CI enforcement: 4 enforcement behaviours (fail-closed default for absent/unrecognised; `onboarded` -> log-entry-modified + target-match; `onboarded-operator-bump` -> log entry + `STATUS.md` `TF-024X-renovate-<slug>` row matching invariant 2 regex literally; `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` -> `DISPATCH-NOTE` `TF-024X-conflict-<adopter>` reference + log file NOT modified) + explicit tooling-slice opt-in gate for `cascade-status: not applicable` (`--allow-not-applicable`; default refusal for direct CLI use). Regex via `python3 re.search()` (PCRE-style); exactly-one-match assertions on both `cascade-status` and `**Target:**` extraction close multi-occurrence injection bypasses; adopter-slug canonicalisation per plan-doc spec. CLI tool only - GitHub Actions workflow wiring (`.github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml`) defers to slice 024B-extras alongside `enable-required-check.sh --restore` mode + ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass procedure. D-044 was reserved for slice 024B at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc 024A merge (PR #102, 2026-05-04); after the operator-decided scope-split (SCOPE-CORRECTION-2026-05-09.md), D-044 stays scoped to core deliverables; sibling D-NNN may be reserved at 024B-extras authoring time for the deferred surface (`--restore` + break-glass + workflow wiring). Slice 024B-core ships under documented protocol deviation per feedback_four_tier_dispatch.md note + justify rule (kernel hook lives in ACC repo only; per-repo install pattern is FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 - open). | ACCEPTED | D-044 was reserved at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc 024A merge per the §9 reservation block. The original 024B accumulated 7 fix-rounds + 7 R-rounds against a single branch with each round closing 5-6 MAJ but introducing 2-3 new defects in just-added code (R6 missed primary-deliverable workflow wiring; R7 fix introduced bootstrap defect on slice's own PR). Empirical signal: surface area too large (16 files, ~3500 lines added) for single-pass convergence. Operator decided 2026-05-09 to split 024B into 024B-core (this slice; ~5 files; scaffolder + template + CI enforcement script + tests) + 024B-extras (deferred; `--restore` mode + ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass + `.github/workflows/` CI wiring + step 7 operator-interactive demo). Split is incremental delivery, NOT descope - both halves still ship; both required before 024C cohort onboarding opens. Original 024B work preserved at `feature/wp-scp-024-024b-extras-parking` branch (audit trail: 7 codex-fix-round-N-audit.log files + 7 R-round archive directories incl r8-archive carry-forward findings). Slice 024B-core scope is internally coherent: scaffolder generates adopter artefacts; CI enforcement script (CLI) validates cascade-status declarations; tests cover both. The CLI script can be invoked manually as a stop-gap until 024B-extras wires it into GitHub Actions. The CLI guard is necessary-but-not-sufficient: workflow-side hardcoding of `--tooling-slice-id` and omission of `--allow-not-applicable` in 024B-extras is the canonical closure path for the operator-supplied not-applicable bypass surface (see FUP-024B-CORE-NOT-APPLICABLE-WORKFLOW-001). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged (D-031); 2026-07-21 quarterly review covers. Identified at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc §9 D-044 reservation |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:97:| D-047 | 2026-05-10 | Adopt WP-SCP-024 024B-extras operational contracts: `scripts/enable-required-check.sh --restore <pre-state.json>` restores branch protection from the captured prior-state log entry by transforming GET-shape JSON into the PUT body, stripping envelope fields recursively, converting `enforce_admins.enabled` to a boolean, and replaying `required_signatures` through the dedicated sub-resource; restore-mode posture-degradation flags `--i-understand-restore-removes-admin-enforcement`, `--i-understand-restore-removes-required-checks`, `--i-understand-restore-re-enables-force-pushes`, and `--i-understand-restore-re-enables-deletions` are mandatory when the target removes or re-enables those protections; forward-mode now performs a path-pinned workflow lookup for `.github/workflows/policy-check-wrapper.yml`, queries workflow-runs without a branch filter, validates `--expected-wrapper-sha` against actual release tags via `gh api repos/jrnb2024/standards-control-plane/git/ref/tags/<tag>`, and treats the working-tree diff of `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md` as part of prior-entry detection; the new CI workflow `.github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml` wires the CLI gate into PR checks with `paths: docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024[C-Z]*/**` plus explicit branch-protection-log.md / STATUS.md triggers and tooling-slice short-circuiting; ADOPT-001 gains §12.8 break-glass procedure and explicitly names the `--i-understand-no-gate-2-verification` escape hatch; STATUS.md and the DISPATCH-NOTE placeholder for the step-7 operator demo are updated in-slice. | ACCEPTED | The 024B split left the restore / break-glass / workflow-integration surface as the deferred half so the review load stayed bounded. This decision ratifies the operational contract that lets operators disable and re-enable the federation primitive safely without widening the adopter-facing API surface, while preserving the bootstrap guard that prevents tooling slices from self-blocking their own PRs. The release-tag SHA requirement is intentionally anchored to actual git tags, not arbitrary commits, so Gate 3 can re-arm against a concrete release artifact rather than against moving main-head. Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: the helper remains operator-run, the escape hatch is explicitly auditable, and the restore demo evidence is still an operator-interactive step outside Codex dispatch. Identified at WP-SCP-024 024B-extras authoring time. |\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/r1-completeness_governance.json:1:{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":525013,\"duration_api_ms\":523855,\"num_turns\":38,\"result\":\"I now have all the information needed to compile the review. Let me emit the structured findings report.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"lens\\\": \\\"completeness_governance\\\",\\n  \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"BLOCKED\\\",\\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"024B-extras completeness governance review: 1 MAJ, 2 MIN, 1 nit. DISPATCH-NOTE ACs are mapped. Step-7 demo AC correctly unchecked at R1; TF-024B-STEP7-DEMO-001 provides merge-blocking tracking in STATUS.md and the placeholder. Plan-doc invariant 2 (CI-enforced) is closed by check-invocation-log-entry.yml: branch-protection-log edit without a cascade DISPATCH-NOTE triggers CI failure; tooling-slice short-circuit covers the R8 bootstrap defect; paths trigger correctly excludes 024A/024B-core/024B-extras. Plan-doc invariant 7 (rollback SLO): --restore mode implemented, APPLY_FAIL pattern tested (failed PUT still emits log; run_restore_case restore-put-fail), all posture-degradation flags enforced and tested, required_signatures POST/DELETE 404-suppression tested, restore-roundtrip-evidence-PLACEHOLDER.md is correctly merge-blocking. R8-SAFE-001 mitigated: WARNING and CAUTION present in §12.8 Gate 3, CAUTION log-block line in script, D-047 rationale acknowledges escape hatch. R8-SAFE-002 mitigated: working-tree diff in prior_restore_evidence_present(). R8-CORR-002 mitigated: check_forward_mode_safety() gated by RESTORE_MODE -eq 0. VERSIONING.md protected-flag list includes all 8 new extras flags. D-047 row covers all deliverables, escape hatch, bootstrap guard, cites split. STATUS.md chain entry documents outcome, placeholder reference, parking-branch deletion note. Plan-doc §6 split note correctly states 024C gates on both 024B-core + 024B-extras + FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001. MAJ finding: §12.8 Gate 1 incomplete flag documentation creates break-glass failure risk under rollback time pressure.\\\",\\n  \\\"findings\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CG-R1-MAJ-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"ADOPT-001 §12.8 Gate 1 documents only 2 of 4 required posture-degradation acknowledgement flags, omitting --i-understand-restore-re-enables-force-pushes and --i-understand-restore-re-enables-deletions. The script requires both flags when the pre-state has allow_force_pushes=true or allow_deletions=true (lines 679-694 of enable-required-check.sh).\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"§12.8 Gate 1 enumerates only --i-understand-restore-removes-admin-enforcement and --i-understand-restore-removes-required-checks. The script's posture-degradation checks at lines 679-695 also enforce ACK_RESTORE_FORCE_PUSHES and ACK_RESTORE_DELETIONS separately, each producing exit 2 with an informative error but no §12.8 reference. DISPATCH-NOTE AC requires 'REQUIRED (not advisory) prerequisite checks'; §12.8 violates this for the force-pushes and deletions cases.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"An operator following §12.8 verbatim when restoring a pre-state with allow_force_pushes: true (e.g., a repo that had permissive settings before cascade) will encounter exit 2 with no §12.8 guidance on the required flag. Under <30-min rollback SLO (plan-doc invariant 7), undocumented flag discovery adds resolution latency directly threatening the SLO claim.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add --i-understand-restore-re-enables-force-pushes and --i-understand-restore-re-enables-deletions to the §12.8 Gate 1 flag list, with the same REQUIRED framing as the two documented flags. Mirror the pattern for the CAUTION log-block entry.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CG-R1-MIN-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"DISPATCH-NOTE AC step-7 and TF-024B-STEP7-DEMO-001 STATUS.md row both use 'byte-for-byte match assertion' / 'byte-for-byte match evidence' as the evidence criterion, but the placeholder's step 6 correctly specifies key-field comparison excluding API envelope fields (timestamps, _links, url). These are irreconcilable: a byte-for-byte match is impossible because envelope fields change between API calls.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"DISPATCH-NOTE §Scope (in) step-7 entry: 'verify branch-protection API response matches captured before-state byte-for-byte'. STATUS.md TF-024B-STEP7-DEMO-001: 'commits byte-for-byte match evidence'. restore-roundtrip-evidence-PLACEHOLDER.md step 6: 'verify branch-protection API GET response matches captured before-state on key fields (strict, contexts, enforce_admins.enabled, required_signatures.enabled); API envelope fields (timestamps, _links, url) differ and should be excluded'.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"A reviewer auditing the step-7 evidence file against the DISPATCH-NOTE AC will use 'byte-for-byte' as the pass criterion and could reject a correct demonstration because envelope fields differ. The placeholder is the operator-facing instruction and is correct; the AC and TF row create a false standard that could block an otherwise valid evidence commit.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Update DISPATCH-NOTE AC step-7 text and TF-024B-STEP7-DEMO-001 STATUS.md row to match the placeholder's specification: 'verify key fields (strict, contexts, enforce_admins.enabled, required_signatures.enabled) match via jq comparison; API envelope fields excluded'.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CG-R1-MIN-002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"The workflow's cohort-priority grep uses `024[C-F]` (a closed range matching the four current cohort slices) while the paths trigger uses `024[C-Z]*/**` (an open range covering any future slice from C to Z). The mismatch creates an undocumented implicit assumption: any cohort slice added outside [C-F] (e.g., if 024G naming expands or a second cohort is numbered [H-Z]) falls through to head -n1 ordering instead of the priority-selection branch. No TF tracks this constraint.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"Workflow line 58: `grep -E '^docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024[C-F]/DISPATCH-NOTE.md$'`. Workflow paths trigger line 18: `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024[C-Z]*/**`. Plan-doc §6 scopes cohort to 024C/024D/024E/024F and tooling to 024G only, so [C-F] is currently correct. No TF documents the [C-F] assumption as a forward-compat constraint.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"No current defect: present cohort is [C-F]. Risk is forward-compat: a future plan amendment adding a cohort slice outside [C-F] would silently degrade priority selection on mixed PRs without any visible CI failure. The constraint is invisible to a future dispatch author reading only §6.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Either broaden the grep pattern to `024[C-Z]` to match the paths trigger, or add a DISPATCH-NOTE / plan-doc §6 note explicitly stating 'cohort slices must remain within 024C-024F; the CI workflow cohort-priority grep is scoped to this range and must be updated if the cohort expands'. File as TF or inline note.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CG-R1-nit-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"nit\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"No dedicated test asserts that forward-mode safety-check output is absent when running in --restore mode (the R8-CORR-002 regression guard). Restore-mode tests implicitly verify correct behavior but do not include an explicit negative assertion.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"DISPATCH-NOTE carry-forward: 'R8-CORR-002: prior-entry detection runs outside restore-mode guard — ensure restore-mode guard scoping is tight.' Implementation is correct (check_forward_mode_safety() called only inside `if [ \\\\\\\"$RESTORE_MODE\\\\\\\" -eq 0 ]` at line 568). Test file has run_restore_case and run_restore_admin_posture_case helpers but no case that asserts the absence of forward-mode safety log lines (e.g., 'safety check:') in restore-mode stdout.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add a restore-mode test case that asserts `grep -v 'safety check' stdout` or that the forward-mode safety workflow-lookup call is absent from the fake_gh calls.log, making R8-CORR-002 regression-detectable without manual inspection.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"2d9f6d00-1d3f-4bb8-bcd1-1674097d96cd\",\"total_cost_usd\":1.7630895999999996,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":1368,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":141898,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":2819617,\"output_tokens\":25270,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":141898,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":1,\"output_tokens\":4232,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":146641,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":2638,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":2638},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":1823,\"outputTokens\":22,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.001933,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-sonnet-4-6\":{\"inputTokens\":1368,\"outputTokens\":25270,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":2819617,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":141898,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":1.7611565999999996,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"e38a5045-ef5d-4215-b008-66bc6e912f9b\"}\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/r1-correctness.json:1:{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":1065456,\"duration_api_ms\":1066014,\"num_turns\":24,\"result\":\"```json\\n{\\n  \\\"reviewer\\\": \\\"sonnet-correctness\\\",\\n  \\\"lens\\\": \\\"correctness\\\",\\n  \\\"slice\\\": \\\"WP-SCP-024-024B-extras\\\",\\n  \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"APPROVED_WITH_FINDINGS\\\",\\n  \\\"crit_count\\\": 0,\\n  \\\"maj_count\\\": 2,\\n  \\\"min_count\\\": 1,\\n  \\\"nit_count\\\": 0,\\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"024B-extras correctness review: 0 CRIT, 2 MAJ, 1 MIN. All four carry-forward findings (R8-CORR-001/002/003, R7-BOOTSTRAP) confirmed closed. The --restore mode GET→PUT shape transform is correct: Python helper strips all GET-only fields, coerces enforce_admins.enabled to boolean, handles null required_status_checks, and compacts restrictions arrays. APPLY_FAIL=0 wrap is correctly initialized before the apply block; failed PUT continues to log emission as specified. The four posture-degradation flags are correctly enforced by the script before apply. required_signatures sub-resource handling (POST/DELETE with 404 suppression) is correct. R8-CORR-002 is definitively closed: check_forward_mode_safety() is called only when RESTORE_MODE -eq 0, so prior-entry detection cannot run in restore context. The workflow path filter (024[C-Z]*/**) plus job-body short-circuit correctly excludes tooling slices without the paths-ignore approach. CORR-001 (MAJ): validate_expected_wrapper_sha_against_tags executes and emits a success log before the prior_restore_evidence_present guard fires; an operator sees 'SHA validated against release tag' immediately before an unrelated exit 2, which is misleading and wastes N+1 GitHub API calls. The test enable-expected-sha-cross-target explicitly asserts this misleading stdout is present, confirming the ordering is implemented but not intentionally designed. CORR-002 (MAJ): ADOPT-001 §12.8 Gate 1 documents only 2 of 4 posture-degradation flags; --i-understand-restore-re-enables-force-pushes and --i-understand-restore-re-enables-deletions are absent from the playbook. An operator restoring to a pre-state with allow_force_pushes=true would hit an unexplained exit 2 mid-incident, directly violating the <30-min SLO. The script correctly enforces both flags; only the documentation is missing. CORR-003 (MIN): run_workflow_guard_case uses an inline bash snippet that lacks the cohort-preference case statement and tooling short-circuit from the real YAML; mixed-PR scenarios and the 024[C-F] preference branch are untested.\\\",\\n  \\\"carry_forward_verification\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R8-CORR-001\\\",\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"CLOSED\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"check-invocation-log-entry.sh uses single-pass ^cascade-status:\\\\\\\\s+ regex anchored at line start with exactly-one-match assertion (exits 2 if count != 1). No trailing inline prose can be captured.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R8-CORR-002\\\",\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"CLOSED\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"check_forward_mode_safety() is invoked only inside the guard `if [ \\\\\\\"$RESTORE_MODE\\\\\\\" -eq 0 ]` (line 568-570 of enable-required-check.sh). All prior_restore_evidence_present calls and prior-entry detection logic are inside that guard. Restore-mode execution path cannot trigger spurious contradiction messages.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R8-CORR-003\\\",\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"CLOSED\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"Closed in 024B-core per DISPATCH-NOTE. TF-ticket match pattern in check-invocation-log-entry.sh uses spec-compliant slug canonicalisation.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R7-BOOTSTRAP\\\",\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"CLOSED\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"Workflow trigger uses paths: [docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024[C-Z]*/**] which structurally excludes 024A and 024B*. Job-body resolve-dispatch step additionally short-circuits exit 0 for tooling slices (024A, 024B-core, 024B-extras, 024G). Combined, this is functionally equivalent to the paths-ignore approach described in DISPATCH-NOTE and closes the bootstrap defect.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"findings\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CORR-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"validate_expected_wrapper_sha_against_tags is called before prior_restore_evidence_present guard; tag API calls run and success log emitted even when the invocation will immediately fail due to absent restore evidence.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"enable-required-check.sh lines 547-553: validate_expected_wrapper_sha_against_tags($EXPECTED_WRAPPER_SHA) executes unconditionally when --expected-wrapper-sha is set, emitting 'expected wrapper SHA validated against release tag <tag>' to stdout. The prior_restore_evidence_present check fires after, emitting the exit-2 error. Test enable-expected-sha-cross-target (test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh) explicitly asserts expected_stdout contains 'expected wrapper SHA validated' AND exit_code 2 — confirming this ordering is tested as-implemented, not as an intentional design choice.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Operator sees a misleading 'SHA validated against release tag' success message immediately before an unrelated failure. N+1 GitHub API calls (paginated tag list + per-tag object SHA) are wasted. The success log could cause an operator under time pressure to believe the critical Gate 3 step succeeded when the actual error is orthogonal.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Reorder: check prior_restore_evidence_present first; error immediately with 'no prior restore evidence found — --expected-wrapper-sha requires a prior --restore invocation on this repo' if absent; only proceed to validate_expected_wrapper_sha_against_tags if evidence is present. Update test enable-expected-sha-cross-target to assert expected_stdout is empty (or contains only the restore-evidence-missing error).\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CORR-002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"ADOPT-001 §12.8 Gate 1 documents only 2 of 4 posture-degradation acknowledgement flags; --i-understand-restore-re-enables-force-pushes and --i-understand-restore-re-enables-deletions are absent from the operator playbook.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"ADOPT-001 §12.8 lines 1205-1210 lists --i-understand-restore-removes-admin-enforcement and --i-understand-restore-removes-required-checks only. enable-required-check.sh enforces --i-understand-restore-re-enables-force-pushes (line 680) when pre-state allow_force_pushes is true and --i-understand-restore-re-enables-deletions (line 688) when pre-state allow_deletions is true. An operator following §12.8 verbatim on a repo whose captured pre-state has either flag enabled would receive an unexplained exit 2 with no documentation path to resolution.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Playbook-following operator cannot complete Gate 1 rollback; SLO <30 min from operator decision to merge-restored state is directly violated while the operator diagnoses the undocumented exit 2. Severity is MAJ not CRIT because the script behaviour is correct — only the prose documentation is incomplete.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add --i-understand-restore-re-enables-force-pushes and --i-understand-restore-re-enables-deletions to §12.8 Gate 1 flag table with same explanatory format as the existing two entries, noting each is required only when the captured pre-state JSON has the corresponding field set to true. No script changes required; tests already cover this path (test lines 2284-2289).\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CORR-003\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"run_workflow_guard_case test exercises an inline bash snippet that omits the cohort-preference logic and tooling short-circuit present in the real YAML; the 024[C-F] preference branch and mixed-PR scenarios are not tested.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh lines 157-224: run_workflow_guard_case runs an inline bash block (lines 171-186) that emits enforce=true for any dispatch note path, without the cohort-preference grep (024[C-F]/DISPATCH-NOTE.md) or tooling-slice case statement present in check-invocation-log-entry.yml lines 57-74. No test case provides a PR diff containing both a tooling dispatch note (e.g. 024B-extras/DISPATCH-NOTE.md) and a cohort dispatch note (e.g. 024C/DISPATCH-NOTE.md) to verify the preference logic selects the cohort note.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"A bug in the cohort-preference grep pattern would not be caught by the test suite. A mixed-PR scenario could silently enforce on a tooling dispatch note, causing spurious failures on PRs that touch both tooling docs and cohort docs.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add run_workflow_guard_case variants: (a) diff contains only a tooling dispatch note → verify job exits 0; (b) diff contains both tooling and cohort dispatch notes → verify cohort is selected and job proceeds to enforcement. Either extract the resolve-dispatch step logic into a sourced bash function testable in isolation, or add an act/nektos workflow-level test.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"2af16195-fba4-46c9-9b26-2c739c686ecd\",\"total_cost_usd\":1.82164905,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":20,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":149739,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":1314745,\"output_tokens\":37005,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":149739,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":3,\"output_tokens\":2373,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":7381,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":16139,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":16139},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":1753,\"outputTokens\":28,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.001893,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-sonnet-4-6\":{\"inputTokens\":1324,\"outputTokens\":54503,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":1455726,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":149739,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":1.81975605,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"089072a8-3ae9-4dfc-b64a-715b4bc008e5\"}\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:876:   gh api repos/OWNER/NAME/branches/BRANCH/protection/required_status_checks \\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:896:1. **Approving review** from a member listed in your repo's CODEOWNERS for the paths the bypass affects. (This is the adopter's own CODEOWNERS file, not SCP's.) **Minimum-necessary path: `output/findings/waivers.json`** — every bypass PR adds a waiver entry per Gate 3 below, so CODEOWNERS coverage on this path guarantees Gate 1 fires on every bypass PR. Extend coverage to `services.yml`, `policies/**` (if you mirror policies locally), and any other SCP-governed paths in your repo, but do NOT omit `waivers.json` — without it, a bypass PR that touches only the wrapper or only a non-listed path slips through Gate 1.\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:900:`scripts/verify-bypass-pairing.sh` (in the SCP repo, called by the workflow's `bypass-gate` step) checks Gates 2 and 3. Gate 1 (CODEOWNERS approval) is enforced by GitHub branch protection — see the two-mode breakdown immediately below. Any miss → `SCP-E004` → merge-blocked.\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:902:**Two operating modes — Gate 1 enforcement varies.** Per D-033, the SCP self-dogfood gate runs in single-operator mode with `required_approving_review_count = 0` because GitHub forbids PR authors from approving their own PRs (count=1 + a single CODEOWNER would lock the operator out entirely). Adopters inherit the same dichotomy:\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:904:- **Multi-maintainer adopters.** Configure branch protection with `required_approving_review_count >= 1`, `require_code_owner_reviews: true`, **AND `dismiss_stale_reviews: true`**. (GitHub enforces non-self-approval at the platform level — there is no separate REST API field for that constraint; the WP-SCP-020 plan corpus uses `require_review_from_non_author: true` as shorthand for this platform behaviour, but no API field needs to be set.) Gate 1 is **machine-enforced** — a bypass PR cannot merge without a CODEOWNER review from someone other than the author. The `dismiss_stale_reviews: true` setting is non-optional in this mode: without it, an approving CODEOWNER review on an initial commit persists across subsequent commits in the same PR, so an attacker who obtains a clean review can later push commits that add `.scp/rule-config.yaml disable: true`, change `scp_bypass: true`, or weaken the wrapper's fork-PR `if:` — and merge without re-review. Dismissal fires on ANY push to the PR branch regardless of which files the new commit touches — it is not path-selective. Stale-review dismissal is the enforcement complement to `require_code_owner_reviews`. The `enable-required-check.sh` helper preserves your existing review-shape verbatim and does NOT set `dismiss_stale_reviews: true` for you; configure it explicitly via the GitHub branch-protection UI or API. (As of fix-round-5 of this slice, `enable-required-check.sh` emits a stderr WARNING when the preserved existing review config has `dismiss_stale_reviews: false` — see also the \"verify the gate is live\" block in §12.7.3.)\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:905:- **Single-operator adopters** (personal account, sole maintainer). Configure `required_approving_review_count = 0` per D-033 (count >= 1 makes the repo unmergeable). Gate 1 collapses to **documentation-only** — `scripts/verify-bypass-pairing.sh` does NOT check for an approving review, and the operator can self-author a bypass PR satisfying only Gates 2 and 3. This is the accepted bus-factor-1 cost (WP-SCP-020 §8 risk row); revisit at the 2026-07-21 quarterly review when a second maintainer onboards.\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:938:   gh api repos/OWNER/NAME/branches/BRANCH/protection/required_status_checks --jq '.contexts'\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:945:   gh api -X PATCH repos/OWNER/NAME/branches/BRANCH/protection/required_status_checks \\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:951:   gh api -X PATCH repos/OWNER/NAME/branches/BRANCH/protection/required_status_checks \\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:1191:### 12.8 Break-glass procedure for federation-primitive failure\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:1195:#### Gate 1 - DISABLE\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:1230:    --expected-wrapper-sha <sha-from-Gate-2>\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:1233:When prior restore evidence exists, the helper requires `--expected-wrapper-sha` and validates that the SHA resolves to an actual release tag in the SCP repo. If the flag is omitted in that state, re-enable is rejected with exit 2.\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:1246:Reference: D-047, WP-SCP-024 invariant 7, D-035, D-030, and the break-glass guidance in `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/DISPATCH-NOTE.md`.\nscripts/enable-required-check.sh:8:#   - required_status_checks.contexts = [\"${SCP_REQUIRED_CONTEXT}\"]\nscripts/enable-required-check.sh:11:#   - required_status_checks.strict = true (PR must be up-to-date\nscripts/enable-required-check.sh:158:  --expected-wrapper-sha SHA\nscripts/enable-required-check.sh:170:  --i-understand-restore-re-enables-force-pushes\nscripts/enable-required-check.sh:173:  --i-understand-restore-re-enables-deletions\nscripts/enable-required-check.sh:212:    --expected-wrapper-sha)\nscripts/enable-required-check.sh:213:      [ $# -lt 2 ] && { echo \"error: --expected-wrapper-sha requires a value\" >&2; usage; exit 2; }\nscripts/enable-required-check.sh:219:    --i-understand-restore-re-enables-force-pushes) ACK_RESTORE_FORCE_PUSHES=1; shift ;;\nscripts/enable-required-check.sh:220:    --i-understand-restore-re-enables-deletions) ACK_RESTORE_DELETIONS=1; shift ;;\nscripts/enable-required-check.sh:251:    echo \"error: --restore cannot be combined with --expected-wrapper-sha\" >&2\nscripts/enable-required-check.sh:265:  echo \"error: --expected-wrapper-sha cannot be combined with --i-understand-no-gate-2-verification\" >&2\nscripts/enable-required-check.sh:270:  echo \"error: --expected-wrapper-sha cannot be combined with --i-understand-this-repo-has-no-prior-green-ci\" >&2\nscripts/enable-required-check.sh:345:validate_restore_source_json() {\nscripts/enable-required-check.sh:364:    \"required_status_checks\": (dict, type(None)),\nscripts/enable-required-check.sh:401:            elif key == \"required_status_checks\" and value is None:\nscripts/enable-required-check.sh:458:prior_restore_evidence_present() {\nscripts/enable-required-check.sh:500:    echo \"error: --expected-wrapper-sha '$expected_sha' was not found in the release-tag SHA cache\" >&2\nscripts/enable-required-check.sh:510:    if prior_restore_evidence_present \"docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md\" \"${REPO}@${BRANCH}\"; then\nscripts/enable-required-check.sh:512:      echo \"       the cold-start escape hatch is for adopters without history; prior-restore evidence indicates Gate 3 (post-break-glass re-enable) — use --expected-wrapper-sha or --i-understand-no-gate-2-verification\" >&2\nscripts/enable-required-check.sh:539:  if prior_restore_evidence_present \"docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch